WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.472
Over the last few years,

00:00:01.552 --> 00:00:06.643
infinite-width theory has given us remarkably clean
answers about deep networks.

00:00:06.723 --> 00:00:11.233
Tools like the Neural Tangent Kernel tell us how a
network initialises,

00:00:11.313 --> 00:00:14.465
how it trains, and where gradient descent ends up.

00:00:14.545 --> 00:00:18.538
But there is a catch: all of this describes a
network's output.

00:00:18.618 --> 00:00:22.288
It says nothing about the network's input-output Jacobian,

00:00:22.368 --> 00:00:25.585
the object that captures how smooth the network is.

00:00:25.665 --> 00:00:32.308
And smoothness is exactly what we care about when
we want robustness to noise or to adversarial attacks.

00:00:32.388 --> 00:00:38.772
So a natural question is left wide open: can we
extend infinite-width theory to the Jacobian itself,

00:00:38.852 --> 00:00:41.940
and to training that deliberately regularises it?
